Chapter: GroundPlanSpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Frame
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Ground
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter GroundPlan
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 GroundPlan: the pavement, cut into chunks along each segment and stitched across
 each joint.

 THE CHUNK COUNT IS A CEILING FLOORED AT ONE, and both halves are graded. Codex's
 foreword has a floor and no ceiling, so the chapter writes `ceil x = -floor(-x)`
 rather than inventing a second rounding rule -- which means a length that lands
 exactly on a chunk boundary must give exactly that many chunks and not one more.
 A segment shorter than a chunk still gets one, and so does a length of zero,
 which is what stops a division by the count.

 EACH SLICE IS CUT BY INDEX AND NOT ACCUMULATED. `a0` is `len * i / n` and `a1`
 is `len * (i+1) / n`, so the last slice ends exactly on the segment end however
 many chunks there are. Adding a chunk at a time would drift, and the drift shows
 up as a seam at the far end of a long segment rather than anywhere near the
 rider. The first slice's near edge and the last slice's far edge are graded
 against the segment itself.

 THE FORWARDS CARRY THE GAME'S COSINE. `to-rider` multiplies the along
 difference by `r-cos` of the rider's yaw, which is 0.999999943741051 at zero
 rather than 1, so a point twenty-five metres ahead reads 24.99999859. Graded a
 step out from that; the ACROSS values are exact, because the sine of zero is
 exactly zero.

 We say:

Section: The Chunking

  fixed-got : List Real
  fixed-got = [road-chunk, entry-road-dist]

  fixed-want : List Real
  fixed-want = [25.0, 40.0]

  colour-got : List Integer
  colour-got = [road-color]

  colour-want : List Integer
  colour-want = [3421500]

 On a boundary it is exact; just past one it rolls over; below one chunk and at
 zero it is still one.

  chunks-got : List Integer
  chunks-got =
    [ chunks-for 25.0, chunks-for 25.1, chunks-for 50.0, chunks-for 49.9,
      chunks-for 1.0, chunks-for 0.0, chunks-for 500.0, chunks-for 320.0,
      chunks-for 1200.0 ]

  chunks-want : List Integer
  chunks-want = [1, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 20, 13, 48]

Section: One Slice

  segs : List Segment
  segs = [segment-at 0, segment-at 1, segment-at 2]

  chain : List Integer
  chain = [0, 1, 2]

  origin : Pose
  origin = Pose { along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }

 Four corners, walked near-left, near-right, far-right, far-left -- so the quad
 closes rather than crossing itself.

  quad : List RiderPt
  quad = slice-quad segs chain origin 0 0 20 500.0 4.0

  quad-shape-got : List Integer
  quad-shape-got = [list-length quad, list-length (slice-quad segs chain origin 0 19 20 500.0 4.0)]

  quad-shape-want : List Integer
  quad-shape-want = [4, 4]

 The across values are exact: the lane is four metres wide and the rider sits on
 its centre line, so its edges are two either side of him.

  across-got : List Real
  across-got =
    [ (list-at quad 0).right, (list-at quad 1).right,
      (list-at quad 2).right, (list-at quad 3).right ]

  across-want : List Real
  across-want = [-2.0, 2.0, 2.0, -2.0]

 The first slice starts at the rider and runs one twentieth of five hundred
 metres; the last ends exactly on the segment's end.

  along-got : List Real
  along-got =
    let last = slice-quad segs chain origin 0 19 20 500.0 4.0
    in [ (list-at quad 0).forward, (list-at quad 3).forward,
         (list-at last 0).forward, (list-at last 3).forward ]

  along-want : List Real
  along-want = [0.0, 25.0, 475.0, 500.0]

 CONSECUTIVE SLICES SHARE AN EDGE EXACTLY, which is what makes the strip read as
 one road. Slice i's far edge is slice i+1's near edge, and that holds because
 both are computed from the index rather than accumulated.

  seam-got : List Real
  seam-got =
    let a = slice-quad segs chain origin 0 3 20 500.0 4.0
    in let b = slice-quad segs chain origin 0 4 20 500.0 4.0
    in [ (list-at a 3).forward - (list-at b 0).forward,
         (list-at a 2).forward - (list-at b 1).forward,
         (list-at a 3).right - (list-at b 0).right ]

  seam-want : List Real
  seam-want = [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

Section: The Strip

 One command per chunk that survives the near clip, and the count never exceeds
 the chunk count.

  strip : List DrawCmd
  strip = seg-road segs chain origin 0 500.0 960.0

  strip-got : List Boolean
  strip-got =
    [ list-length strip > 0,
      list-length strip <= chunks-for 500.0,
      list-length (seg-road segs chain (Pose { along = 5000.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }) 0 500.0 960.0) == 0 ]

  strip-want : List Boolean
  strip-want = [True, True, True]

 Every command is a flat polygon in the road colour, with four vertices flattened
 to eight reals -- or fewer, where the near plane clipped one.

  cmd-got : List Integer
  cmd-got =
    let c = list-at strip (list-length strip - 1)
    in [c.tag, c.color, c.color2, list-length c.geom]

  cmd-want : List Integer
  cmd-want = [0, 3421500, 0, 0]

  points-got : List Boolean
  points-got =
    let c = list-at strip (list-length strip - 1)
    in [list-length c.pts >= 6, list-length c.pts <= 10]

  points-want : List Boolean
  points-want = [True, True]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "gp-fixed " fixed-got fixed-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "gp-colour" colour-got colour-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "gp-chunks" chunks-got chunks-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "gp-shape " quad-shape-got quad-shape-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "gp-across" across-got across-want 0.0001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "gp-along " along-got along-want 0.0001)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "gp-seam  " seam-got seam-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "gp-strip " strip-got strip-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "gp-cmd   " cmd-got cmd-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "gp-points" points-got points-want)
  end
